#!/bin/sh
# managed by proteus — Alpine post-install scriptlet
#
# Runs once on `apk add proteus`. Keep it short: Alpine packagers
# discourage interactive prompts here, and we don't want to enable
# anything implicitly. The actual rotation cadence comes from the
# OpenRC periodic shims (see dist/openrc/), not from this script.

cat <<'EOF'
proteus has been installed.

Run `proteus doctor` to verify your init system, libc, and backend are
all detected correctly. Then:

  rc-service proteus start         # apply config + first rotation
  rc-update add proteus default    # enable on boot

Periodic rotation is wired via /etc/periodic/hourly/proteus-rotate; the
OpenRC `cron` provider must be running for it to fire (`rc-service crond
start`). See `proteus wiki rotation` for tuning.

NOTE (B5 / N12.8): Alpine ships only the OpenRC service tree. The
event-driven rotation daemon (proteus-events.service) is a systemd unit
and is NOT installed on Alpine — its closest analogue is the existing
NetworkManager dispatcher hook under /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/.
EOF
